WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.760
Coily hair, wash day, and a clean, safe workstation.

2
00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.950
This is the video for module one of Hair Care and Simple Styling.

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:20.150
Afro-textured hair is not hard or bad hair. The part you see is dead protein called keratin, so once it is damaged, it cannot heal. Prevention is everything.

4
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:28.690
On tightly coiled hair, the scalp's natural oil, sebum, struggles to travel past each bend, so the ends stay dry.

5
00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.380
Every bend is a weak point. Combed dry, pulled tight or brushed hard, hair snaps there.

6
00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:41.130
So keep three rules: moisture, gentle detangling, and low tension.

7
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:50.090
For example, Amaka in Onitsha takes down Blessing's braids after seven weeks, and Blessing is worried by the hair in the comb.

8
00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:56.960
Shed hairs have a tiny white bulb at the root end. Some shedding every day is normal, and braids trap it.

9
00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:09.580
Short pieces with no bulb are broken. Most came from the nape, where the braids were smallest and driest. So next time, Amaka makes the nape braids looser and slightly bigger.

10
00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:18.070
Wash about once every one to two weeks. Step one. Divide the hair into four to six sections, to prevent matting.

11
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:26.040
Step two. Shampoo the scalp with the pads of your fingers, not your nails, and let the lather run down the length.

12
00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:32.490
Step three. Put conditioner on the length and ends, and leave it for the time on the label.

13
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:39.440
Step four. Detangle while the conditioner is still in. Fingers first, then a wide-tooth comb.

14
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:46.470
This is the rule. Start at the ends and work up to the roots. Never comb coily hair dry.

15
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:53.860
Step five. Rinse with cool or lukewarm water, then blot with a cotton T-shirt instead of rubbing.

16
00:01:55.110 --> 00:02:02.220
Step six. Moisturise and seal. Water is the true moisturiser; oils and butters slow water loss.

17
00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:09.880
LOC means liquid, then oil, then cream. Low porosity hair often prefers LCO, with oil last.

18
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:19.950
Combs, clips and towels can carry lice, ringworm and bacteria from head to head. After every client, remove all hair from your tools.

19
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.660
Wash them in warm water with liquid soap, scrubbing between the teeth with an old toothbrush.

20
00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:32.320
Cleaning comes first, because disinfectant cannot work through grease and hair.

21
00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:45.330
Then fully soak the tools in a salon disinfectant mixed exactly as the label says. Barbicide's label contact time is 10 minutes. For other products, follow their label.

22
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:54.920
Store them in a clean, closed container. Keep two sets, so one is always clean, and use a fresh towel and cape for every client.

23
00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:05.850
Store them in a clean, closed container. Keep two sets, so one is always clean while the other soaks. And use a fresh towel and cape for every client.

24
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:17.880
With household bleach, dilute exactly as the bottle says, mix fresh each day, wear gloves and open a window. Never mix it with ammonia, vinegar or other cleaners.

25
00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:26.720
To seal braid ends, pour hot water into a stable bowl. Never hold the kettle over the client, and keep children away.

26
00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:35.820
If you see lice, scaly patches, sores or pus, stop politely, suggest a clinic, and disinfect every tool you used.

27
00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:45.140
Here is a worked example. Ngozi in Enugu wants to braid on Saturdays in her parlour, with about 30,000 naira to spend.

28
00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:54.810
Combs and clips cost about 6,000 naira. Three capes and six towels, so she can rotate them, cost about 12,000.

29
00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:05.710
A lidded container and disinfectant cost about 9,000, and she keeps 3,000 for soap, gloves and a lidded bin. Prices are approximate.

30
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.330
That is her 30,000 naira: two sets of tools, and clean linen for every client.

31
00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:26.890
To remember. Prevention is everything. Keep to moisture, gentle detangling and low tension. Water hydrates, oils seal. Clean before you disinfect. And never style over signs of infection.

32
00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:34.100
Now practise. Read three heads of hair, do one full wash day, and build your hygiene station.

33
00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:36.390
Continue with the lessons below.
